==============================================================================
  1.  WHAT DID TIMING ALIGNMENT ACTUALLY BUY?  (measured, not assumed)
==============================================================================
  fan_on  B=30 blocks | cross-block coherence gamma:  near 0.4-4 m 0.859   far >60 m (noise) 0.0245   (1/B = 0.0333)
  fan_off B=20 blocks | cross-block coherence gamma:  near 0.4-4 m 0.836   far >60 m (noise) 0.0398   (1/B = 0.0500)
  box_in  B=20 blocks | cross-block coherence gamma:  near 0.4-4 m 0.657   far >60 m (noise) 0.0373   (1/B = 0.0500)
  moving  B=30 blocks | cross-block coherence gamma:  near 0.4-4 m 0.507   far >60 m (noise) 0.0243   (1/B = 0.0333)

  gamma ~ 1 means the complex range profile repeats bit-for-bit across
  INDEPENDENT triggered acquisitions -- the timing alignment is real and
  slow-time phase is meaningful.  gamma ~ 1/B is what a random start phase
  would give.  Coherent integration over B=30 blocks therefore buys
  10log10(30) = 14.8 dB on static content versus 10log10(sqrt(30)) = 7.4 dB
  for incoherent averaging: 7.4 dB of EXTRA gain -- but only on static content.


